{
 "experiment": "E-INC-2c",
 "question": "when the supersession lands late (cycle 5), is the old decision retired while the new one is kept?",
 "plantCycle": 5,
 "answer": "The new decision is kept in both placements, but the old decision is NOT retired in either: it stays in DECISIONS (current) with its anchor at every cycle after planting. The ledger's supersession misfires the same way early and late.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "id": "W9-F4",
   "title": "supersession retires the corrections, not the old decision (mutual retirement misfire)",
   "evidence": "The retirement loop scores candidates by shared specific tokens; the two CORRECTION lines share more tokens with each other (correction/architecture/module/instead plus the 'CONSTRAINT'-style scaffold words) than either shares with the old 'Decision: use architecture A ...' line, so they retire each other. The rendered ledger lists 'C10 replaced by C8: CORRECTION ...' and 'C8 replaced by C10: CORRECTION ...' while D5/D6 ('use architecture A ...') remain under DECISIONS (current)."
  },
  {
   "id": "W9-F5",
   "title": "late supersession costs one extra constraint anchor vs early (id-renumbering again)",
   "evidence": "w9-a-latecorr5c retains 15/21 vs 16/21 for the early baseline w9-a-8c-retired; the only difference is q-constraint-6 ('preserve the auth audit trail verbatim'). Moving the corrections to cycle 5 shifts the auto-numbered constraint ids at the merge boundary, and the carried retired-id set deletes the renumbered item (same mechanism as W9-F2)."
  }
 ],
